{#
  Benchmark harness dispatcher -- the single include point op templates use
  (e.g. `{% include "common/standalone/benchmark.j2" %}` in
  ConvolutionFunctions/convolve/convolve.c.j2). Op templates stay agnostic to
  which target is active; they only need to define `{{name}}_bench_init()` /
  `{{name}}_bench_op()` and set `supports_benchmark = true` before including
  common/standalone/main.j2.

  There is one backend now: benchmark_fvp.j2 (DWT cycle counter, works on
  cortex-m4/m55, not cycle accurate under FVP).

  The second backend, benchmark_hw.j2, emitted DWT + ARMv8.1-M PMU counters for
  Apollo510 and was selected with `benchmark_target = "hardware"`
  ($HELIA_BENCH_TARGET). It is gone. Generated standalone harnesses are built
  only as FVP executables -- they link retarget/cmsis_startup against the
  Corstone-300 linker script -- so the hardware backend's am_mcu_apollo.h /
  pmu_armv8.h includes had no include path to resolve against and the harness
  could not compile. Its replacement is `helia_core_tester hardware run`, which
  measures the same kernels on real silicon through the benchmark-server
  firmware and reads far more of the PMU than this ever did (see
  benchmark_server_session.c and docs/performance-streaming-design.md).
  `benchmark_target` is validated at generation time, so asking for "hardware"
  is an error there rather than a compile failure here.

  Kept as a dispatcher rather than collapsed into the op templates so a future
  backend is one branch again, and so the include line in every op template
  stays put.
#}
{% include "common/standalone/benchmark_fvp.j2" %}
